#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: ggdef
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [move, liveness, loop]
# doc: |
#   RV-F #13 (loop revive-seeding, ACCEPT direction). `n` is declared in an
#   ENCLOSING scope, then inside a 2-element loop it is FIRST whole-local
#   reassigned (`n = "fresh"`) and only THEN moved (`consume(!n)`). The reassign
#   re-creates the value for THIS iteration, so the move is legal every iteration
#   — no use-after-move on the next one. This works because a whole-local rebind
#   inside a loop seeds the innermost loop-local set (production `origins.rs:18`);
#   the oracle previously revived the moved slot but did NOT re-seed it loop-local,
#   so it wrongly rejected this program with E_MoveInLoop. Contrast
#   reject_move_in_loop.gg: there the enclosing `n` is moved with NO intervening
#   reassign, so the second iteration genuinely re-moves a dead slot.
# expect:
#   exit: 0
#   stdout: "done\n"
#!end

void consume(String !s):
    pass

void main():
    String n = "outer"
    Vector[int] items = [1, 2]
    for i in items:
        n = "fresh"
        consume(!n)
    print("done")
